00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:57.330
They've been awesome
to us as partners.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:59.460
We have literally
two, three pages

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.970
worth of requests or problems
that we're trying to solve.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:04.740
And Trish and Juan
over there have

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:08.140
been awesome being able to
solve all of our problems.

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:11.910
The other reason why we
also use Google Cloud--

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:15.690
we tested out several
different cloud platforms.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:16.950
I can't mention their names.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:18.630
But, at the end of
the day, it came down

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:19.920
to speed and reliability.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:21.599
And Google won hands-down.

00:07:21.599 --> 00:07:22.890
There was no question about it.

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:25.760
There was-- when you're
dealing with video,

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:29.610
such a high, intense
kind of data flow,

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:33.720
there's not one
dropped packet or, oh,

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:36.450
it's 10 seconds too late
by the time it gets there.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:37.960
We can't react quick enough.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:43.840
And so that's why we chose
Google as our cloud partner.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:44.340
Cool.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.920
So, a quick overview
of our solution.

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:49.160
We have a variety
of methods of input,

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:50.990
I guess, is the best
way of putting it.

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:53.840
So we have a mobile app, which
is mostly used by our security

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:55.382
guards, just roaming
security guards,

00:07:55.382 --> 00:07:57.590
running around, trying to
figure out what's going on.

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:00.500
They'll get notifications and
alarms to their cell phone.

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:01.459
We have our web client.

00:08:01.459 --> 00:08:03.499
A lot of different things
are done through there.

00:08:03.499 --> 00:08:04.580
Pretty much the entire--

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:06.246
anything you can do
in our thick client,

00:08:06.246 --> 00:08:07.837
you can also do
in our web client.

00:08:07.837 --> 00:08:09.920
We have a thick client,
which is more traditional.

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:15.934
That's where you have kind
of your normal sort of--

00:08:15.934 --> 00:08:17.100
trying to think of the word.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:17.933
I can't remember it.

00:08:17.933 --> 00:08:18.671
I'm sorry.

00:08:18.671 --> 00:08:19.170
Huh?

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:20.090
- Security operations.

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:20.960
BEN ROWE: Security
operations center--

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:22.043
I can't remember the word.

00:08:22.043 --> 00:08:24.190
But it's all good.

00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:27.750
So mostly they'll be
using that product.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:30.560
High, lots of volume video,
normally 32 cameras up

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:32.240
on one big screen.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.390
And then, kind of get down
into the IoT side of things,

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:37.450
and the actual
device side, right?

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:40.919
So, cameras-- as
we are effectively

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:43.250
out of incubation
for milestone, we

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:45.660
actually support
about 6,000 cameras,

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:47.510
primarily through
the ONVIF protocol,

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:49.860
which, if you're familiar
with security cameras,

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:53.870
that's very large for us, along
with a variety of very specific

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:55.520
device drivers for the cameras.

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:59.300
Our IoT device platform
is completely extensible.

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:01.310
Although it's not fully
open-platform yet,

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:03.110
it will eventually
be open platform,

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:05.407
so we can plug into
any IoT vendor on prem,

00:09:05.407 --> 00:09:07.490
as well as potentially
cloud-to-cloud integrations

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:09.032
in the future.

00:09:09.032 --> 00:09:10.490
Access control is
another huge one,

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:12.980
obviously being able to react,
lock down doors or open up

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:14.906
doors, or video
verification-- hey,

00:09:14.906 --> 00:09:16.280
someone passed
through this door.

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:17.450
Hey, what's going on?

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:19.680
It's pretty interesting.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:22.220
And then, finally, our
third-party integrations.

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:23.570
It's not fully open yet.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:27.300
We are planning on releasing
a fully open API platform.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:30.620
It's very interesting
being able to integrate

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:31.830
with other sources of data.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:32.840
We don't know what they are yet.

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:34.790
But that's where like
being able to pull in

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:36.581
like unstructured data
into structured data

00:09:36.581 --> 00:09:38.654
and having all these
feeds of structured data,

00:09:38.654 --> 00:09:40.070
we can do so much
more, whether it

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:41.840
be analytics over
the top of that

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:46.940
or whether it's just
reactive elements.

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:50.600
OK, so here's our architecture.

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:52.730
GKE in the cloud, obviously,
I talked about before.

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:54.813
And, also, we actually run
Kubernetes at the edge.

00:09:54.813 --> 00:09:57.050
And it's something I kind
of saved for this slide.

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:59.015
Kubernetes is very,
very interesting.

00:09:59.015 --> 00:10:01.480
It allows us to deploy
micro services at the edge,

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:02.480
as well as in the cloud.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.360
And then you're much quicker
at reacting, from a devop

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:07.370
standpoint, being able
to fix certain things,

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:10.460
but also being able to
deploy upgrades on the fly--

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:13.220
they go down, they come up--
with very little interruption

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:13.910
to the customer.

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:16.620
There's no longer you're
downloading firmware updates

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:17.540
or Windows updates.

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:19.998
It's all kind of just done live
and switched hot-swappable.

00:10:19.998 --> 00:10:22.220
It's great.

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:24.140
Being able to do that
on a security platform

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:27.330
is insanely useful, is
the best way I can put it.

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:28.190
Cool.

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:30.710
So, a quick, high-level view.

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:33.117
We have a video micro service
that sits on the edge.

00:10:33.117 --> 00:10:35.200
That's actually where all
our camera drivers live.

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:38.840
So it's all of our data
in, which is unstructured,

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:41.330
which then pushes it
to the video proxy.

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:44.180
Our proxy, then, manages--

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:45.920
we use Google Storage.

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:48.200
We store all that stuff there.

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:54.390
We then have our AI-ML
flow and workflow.

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:56.780
We convert our unstructured
data to structured data,

00:10:56.780 --> 00:10:59.760
as Sheriff said earlier.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:01.010
We also have our IoT devices.

00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:02.660
Again, all the
drivers and devices

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:05.380
live down there, at the edge.

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:08.180
All the devices connect
on prem or on the cloud.

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:09.890
It does live on the
GKE side as well.

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:13.040
But, primarily, right now
it's all at the gateway.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:14.990
We use Cloud IoT Core.

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:18.335
Obviously, that's one
of the big things here.

00:11:18.335 --> 00:11:20.210
It saved us about a
month of development time

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:22.354
building off of IoT Core.

00:11:22.354 --> 00:11:23.270
It's already hardened.

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:24.103
It's already secure.

00:11:24.103 --> 00:11:26.880
We use the MQTT socket base,
if anyone is interested.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:30.170
We don't use HTTP push.

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:33.440
It just had all the features
we needed at the time.

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:35.056
And the fact that
it automatically

00:11:35.056 --> 00:11:37.430
supported private-public key
exchange and all that stuff,

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:39.020
it just made so much sense.

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:43.370
Obviously, with
Cloud IoT, we use

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:45.530
pub-sub to transport
all of our messages,

00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:50.550
similar to RabbitMQ and Kafka,
just messaging platform.

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:52.790
And then the next
one is our processor.

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:56.321
How many of you guys
have heard of GDPR?

00:11:56.321 --> 00:11:57.820
How many of you
have to deal with it

00:11:57.820 --> 00:12:00.234
in like actual, real life?

00:12:00.234 --> 00:12:02.150
So one of the interesting
things with IoT data

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:03.983
is you don't actually
know what kind of data

00:12:03.983 --> 00:12:05.494
is actually coming in.

00:12:05.494 --> 00:12:07.160
We've built a platform
with our drivers.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:10.020
We actually have to categorize
that data before it comes in.

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:11.978
And we actually have to
pause out and make sure

00:12:11.978 --> 00:12:14.280
that we can utilize it
in certain situations.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:16.363
So we've got a whole
framework and legal framework

00:12:16.363 --> 00:12:19.110
around this, separate pieces
which allow us to say, hey,

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.277
if this data is coming
in from Germany,

00:12:21.277 --> 00:12:23.360
it can't leave the country,
so we have to store it

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:24.920
in a German data center--

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:28.460
that kind of rules-based
engine, based on geolocation.

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:30.069
It's pretty intense.

00:12:30.069 --> 00:12:31.360
It was pretty interesting find.

00:12:33.877 --> 00:12:35.460
So that's how we
kind of handled that,

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:37.610
just by classifying the data,
and then being able to move it.

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:38.700
And then, based on
that classification,

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:41.200
depending on what we can do
with it, we can either store it.

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:42.640
We can just drop it.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:45.300
We store all of our
historical data in BigQuery.

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:48.990
That's where we can do
statistical analysis over time.

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:51.450
But we also then like to
pass some of that information

00:12:51.450 --> 00:12:54.060
onto our real-time engine, which
is an in-house built function

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:58.380
as a service style system.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:00.420
We can choose to send
it to this or not.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:02.130
Obviously, anyone
familiar with some

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:03.734
of the legal
ramifications of GDPR,

00:13:03.734 --> 00:13:05.400
you may not be able
to pass it to users.

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:07.140
So we may actually
have to just drop it

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:08.784
before it even gets there.

00:13:08.784 --> 00:13:10.450
So the real-time
engine-- what it can do

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:11.616
are taking multiple sensors.

00:13:11.616 --> 00:13:13.950
So it could be coming
from our AI-ML services.

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:15.660
It could be coming
from our IT services.

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:18.540
And we can kind of, based
on a series set of rules,

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:21.640
or potentially replumb it
back into any kind of engine.

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:25.070
So it's kind of an
anything-to-anything engine.

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:25.890
We have sent away.

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:28.560
But we can kind of take in lots
of different types of data,

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:31.260
and then either reprocess
it in a different form,

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:34.140
or then take that form and
then action upon it back out,

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:38.370
either via an IoT data sensor,
or change a configuration

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:40.830
somewhere on our
platform to help provide

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:41.970
more actionable insights.

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:46.142
[MUSIC PLAYING]

